{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba ../../../AGENTS.md | sed -n '91,119p;145,166p'; nl -ba PAID-DESIGN.md | sed -n '67,215p'; nl -ba codex-r25.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba ../../../AGENTS.md | sed -n '91,119p;145,166p'; nl -ba PAID-DESIGN.md | sed -n '67,215p'; nl -ba codex-r25.md\"","aggregated_output":"    91\t## 2. Before writing code: design\n    92\t\n    93\tCode written before the thinking costs rounds of review. A design holds these tables before code and before any review; a blank cell\n    94\tmeans it is not ready:\n    95\t\n    96\t1. **State × event**, for work that spans more than one step, run or day: every state against every event it can meet (success,\n    97\t   refusal, a refused retry, an external failure, a crash midway, work still in flight when a run ends, a later run on a later date,\n    98\t   configuration changed in between, an input corrected while waiting, another writer of the same data, two scopes at once), including\n    99\t   the work before its first step and after its terminal outcome. Each cell names the transition, what is written, and the date every\n   100\t   read and write uses.\n   101\t2. **Value × reader.** Every value a change writes, against every reader of it (found by searching the field) and every input source the\n   102\t   value can come from. A rule over a kind of data lists every writer of that kind. A change to how often something fires is counted\n   103\t   against every reader of its output.\n   104\t3. **New path × old checks.** For a new input, derived value, mode or changed data shape: every check the old path passes, and whether\n   105\t   the new one meets it. A changed shape re-derives every check written for the old one.\n   106\t4. **Prior decisions.** The decisions searched for the mechanism by name; any decision that rejected or bounded it is quoted in the\n   107\t   design and in the question to Alex.\n   108\t\n   109\t- A contract over text or shapes states the closed set of what passes (a full match); anything else fails or is not judged. Enumerate\n   110\t  the accepted set against every context and reader it meets before the first edit.\n   111\t- **Code owns identity, never importance.** Before building a closed list, enum or gate, write which decision it takes from the model\n   112\t  and whether that decision is identity (\"are these the same thing?\") or judgement (\"does this matter?\"); build only the identity part.\n   113\t  A list of what matters is at most a hint shown to the model (Alex, 2026-10-25: \"你这个做法难道不是非常的限死吗？永远都是一堆财务指标，真正的观点呢？\").\n   114\t- **No case-fitted fix.** A hand list, a special path or a rule fitted to the case in view fixes that case and breaks the next. First ask\n   115\t  whether the judge had the material and the definition it needed, and whether a typed field is missing; run the proposal against the\n   116\t  existing tests and the gold for a counter-example before building (D167: \"我不要 hardcode 解法 it's premature optimization, not general root fix\").\n   117\t- A blind review measures the tables. A serious finding they do not hold shows a missing dimension: add it and walk every row again,\n   118\t  never answer the finding alone. Code starts when the review finds nothing serious. A design whose core contract is still open is a\n   119\t  question, not something to build.\n   145\t## 4. Tests\n   146\t\n   147\t- Every test names the requirement or table row it proves; a test that traces to none is deleted, and a retired behaviour's tests leave\n   148\t  with it. The suite stays lean.\n   149\t- Before writing a test, read every function its fixture drives: return shapes, failure classification, the data and attributes it\n   150\t  reads. Check that a name exists before calling it.\n   151\t- Real objects first (real storage on a temporary path, real records). Fake only external services (a model client, a data source, an\n   152\t  HTTP server). Fixture values are realistic (real ISO timestamps, not placeholders).\n   153\t- Tests never reach a paid service or load a credential: `tests/conftest.py` replaces the model and Jev key readers with a refusal, so a\n   154\t  call that escapes its fake transport fails the test.\n   155\t- Write the one decisive assertion that fails without the code under test, and predict the run before running it. A first failure for\n   156\t  any other reason means the test was written blind: stop and read again. A test is never mended to fit the code; it changes only when\n   157\t  the spec changes, and the spec change is recorded first.\n   158\t- Mutate on a scratch copy: the test must fail on the mutant while the unmutated control passes. An error is not a catch until its\n   159\t  traceback is read. A negative assertion (\"never called\") needs a mutant that makes the call on the tested path.\n   160\t- A check must judge something: it reports how many cases it judged, carries a positive control that fails for the breach it holds, and\n   161\t  fails when its own preconditions fail (exit status, a missing or unidentifiable input). A check over an empty population is\n   162\t  unexercised, not passed.\n   163\t- Tests never depend on the real date or the working directory: pin every date written and read, build paths from the test file's\n   164\t  location, write only inside temporary directories.\n   165\t- Test each entry point as its user calls it (with the defaults the user leaves unset, under the configuration the user runs), not only\n   166\t  the inner function with every argument set.\n    67\t\n    68\t## 4. Reading rule (frozen before the send)\n    69\t\n    70\t**Unit:** a calendar date of the fixed window 07-10..09-20 (73 dates); on each, each question's conclusion is its last accepted\n    71\tjudgement on or before the date, carried forward. A date's class comes from the **distinct answer pair** (v9's and v14's answers) in\n    72\tforce that day.\n    73\t\n    74\t**Hand classification of every distinct pair (round 23):** `contradictions.py` orders the work (definite and ambiguous first); every\n    75\tdistinct pair in all six sequences is read by hand against both answers and classed **definite** (Data Center's annual low end above\n    76\ttotal revenue's high end, as each answer finally states its FY2027 forecast), **overlap**, **clear**, or **unresolved** (an answer\n    77\tstates no FY2027 forecast that can be read). A parser class that the reading changes is recorded as a misparse with its corrected\n    78\tclass. Dates inherit their pair's class. Before reading, the pairs are listed per sequence with their date spans.\n    79\t\n    80\t**Primary line, P, per pair n (round 24: three outcomes):** over all 73 dates, each arm's dates are definite (D), not definite\n    81\t(overlap or clear, N) or unresolved (U), each date keeping its own arm's class even where the other arm is unresolved. Linked-n's\n    82\tdefinite count lies between D_L and D_L + U_L; control-n's between D_C and D_C + U_C.\n    83\t- **Improved:** D_L + U_L < D_C (the least favourable completion still has fewer definite dates in the linked arm).\n    84\t- **Not improved:** D_L >= D_C + U_C (even the most favourable completion cannot give the linked arm fewer).\n    85\t- **Undecided:** otherwise (the unresolved dates could change the result).\n    86\t- **Testable:** D_C >= 2 (known definite dates of the control over all 73, not only dates readable in both arms).\n    87\t- **Readable:** each arm has at least 60 of the 73 dates resolved: a coverage policy that deliberately allows at most 13 unreadable\n    88\t  dates per arm (82.2%); the bounds above decide whether those dates matter.\n    89\t- **Credit (rounds 24–25; one rule):** a **gain** is a date on which the linked arm is not definite and the control is definite. Only\n    90\t  gains are ever adjusted; no other date's class changes for credit. When a pair is provisionally improved, **every** gain is reviewed:\n    91\t  both linked answers in force on it (v9's and v14's), deduplicated by judgement id, are read for July's rule B (arithmetic following\n    92\t  from stated inputs; each assumption that sizes the result disclosed and supported) and for H4. A gain whose forecasts carry a material\n    93\t  rule-B failure on which its reconciliation rests earns no credit (counted definite for P). A gain resting on an unsupported CPU\n    94\t  increment: when the increment can be quantified, the forecast with it removed gives the gain's credit-adjusted class; when it cannot\n    95\t  and could decide the pair, the gain counts unresolved in the bounds. Each date keeps its actual class in the report beside its\n    96\t  credit-adjusted class; P is recomputed on the credit-adjusted classes. A confirmed CPU double count stays a harm (H4) whatever its\n    97\t  date.\n    98\t- Reported apart, per arm: definite dates that became overlap and those that became clear.\n    99\t\n   100\t**Harm lines (each checked on all three pairs):**\n   101\t- **H1, refusals (round 24):** on **matched reader-days** (a reader judged on that day in both arms of the pair), the attempted reader\n   102\t  judgements and the model refusals among them (first attempts the code refused, terminal refusals included); refusals on linked-only\n   103\t  propagation days reported apart. Harm at **4 or more** refusals above its control on matched reader-days (3 extra is tolerated).\n   104\t  Rationale, frozen before the run: old-2's stored run refused 4 of 1,616 judgements (0.25%), about 0.6 expected per ~250 reader-days, so\n   105\t  4 extra is far above that base rate while up to 3 stays inside ordinary variation. A **linked double refusal** (the corrected answer\n   106\t  refused again: `judgement_refused_twice` stops the day) is a product failure: the verdict is **fail** whatever else holds; a control\n   107\t  double refusal makes the comparison invalid.\n   108\t- **H2, the fixed trajectory:** an unexplained difference in a non-reader request or its substantive output makes the comparison\n   109\t  **invalid** (not a harm: the paired design no longer holds).\n   110\t- **H3, sourcing through the link (round 23):** a figure an answer presents as the market's (consensus, guidance, an analyst estimate)\n   111\t  is followed through the exact request: through the dependency block to the read judgement that stated it, and from there to its dated\n   112\t  outside statement (a company figure such as Bloomberg's $393B total-revenue estimate reaching Data Center through total revenue is the\n   113\t  intended use, if it is named as a company figure). A grounding error: a figure supported only by the system's own forecast, or a\n   114\t  company estimate presented as a segment consensus. Checked regardless of keywords: every reader reference whose figure differs from\n   115\t  that reader's previous reference in the sequence in **amount, scope or attribution** (round 24: an unchanged $393B can move from a\n   116\t  company figure to a segment consensus), and every reader answer on the seven fixed dates below; keyword hits elsewhere are read too.\n   117\t  Harm when a linked sequence has a grounding error and its control's conclusion in force on that date (its fresh judgement, or the one\n   118\t  carried forward) does not have the same specific error.\n   119\t- **H4, the CPU link:** every post-link v14 answer in the linked sequences is read against the exact CPU conclusion it received and\n   120\t  its control's v14 answer that day. Double counting the CPU forecast is harm. Its effect on P goes only through the credit rule above.\n   121\t- **H5, arithmetic and sourcing (July's rule B):** v9 and v14 at seven fixed dates (07-12, 07-26, 08-09, 08-23, 09-06, 09-13, 09-20) in\n   122\t  all six sequences; the unit is a question-date; Claude reads first, a blind reader second; a disagreement goes to a third,\n   123\t  source-backed read whose class decides. H5 is a separate paired harm count: a linked sequence with at least 2 more material failures\n   124\t  than its control is harm. Its effect on P goes only through the credit rule above (a shared error moves no date).\n   125\t\n   126\t**Verdict precedence:** (1) a **linked double refusal** → fail; (2) **invalid or incomplete** (an infrastructure stop: budget, transport\n   127\tor harness; a control's double refusal; an H2 difference; an arm under 60 resolved dates) → inconclusive; (3) **confirmed harm** (H1, H3,\n   128\tH4 double count, H5) → fail; (4) **untestable** (a control with D_C < 2) → inconclusive, \"the class did not recur\"; (5) **P**: all three\n   129\tpairs improved → pass; any pair not improved → fail; otherwise (some pair undecided) → inconclusive.\n   130\t\n   131\t**The worksheet (pinned before the launch; round 24):** built by `worksheet.py` from the six stores and archives after the run, its\n   132\tformat and code reviewed and run on the dry outputs before the send. One row per distinct v9/v14 answer pair per sequence, keyed by\n   133\tboth judgement ids and the pair's date span; it carries both answers' full `implied_outcome` and reasons, and fields for the reader:\n   134\tthe FY2027 low and high ends as each answer finally states them, the excerpt they come from, the class, a note. H3, H4 and H5 rows carry\n   135\tthe exact request text (dependency block marked), the read judgement each figure came through, and the dated source quotes. Worked\n   136\texample (old-1 09-08): Data Center \"$419 billion for FY2027\" against total revenue \"$414.3 billion ... supports roughly $414–421\n   137\tbillion\": low end 419 is not above high end 421, so **overlap**, though the parser took $414.3B and flagged it definite. Claude reads\n   138\tfirst; a blind reader (Codex, labels shuffled, key kept outside the repository) second; every disagreement that could change a pair's\n   139\toutcome, an H3 or H4 finding, or an H5 mark goes to a third, source-backed read whose class decides. Reading load on old-2's schedule:\n   140\tabout 72 distinct pairs a sequence (up to 438 rows, each read twice: 876 classifications), about 213 linked v14 reviews (twice where\n   141\tan H4 finding is made), 168 H5 marks (84 question-dates twice), the H3 traces, and the full gain review (both supplying answers of every\n   142\tgain, deduplicated), before adjudication; the dry review counts each reader's rows apart; estimated 8–12 hours of reading across the\n   143\treaders, apart from the run's three hours.\n   144\t\n   145\t**Reported beside, not gating:** each link's relation, direction and sign (the weak CPU link disclosed); self-made references\n   146\t(`reference_census.py`'s measures, the `unsourced` scan with the dependency block excluded); v14 linked against control; v21's implied\n   147\ttotal against v9 and v14; linked-only judgement days.\n   148\t\n   149\t## 5. Cost (estimate; the ceiling is Alex's)\n   150\t\n   151\tReader judgements from their links, at published rates from old-2's archived usage: $0.872 a sequence (247 judgements, round 22).\n   152\tLinked sequences: about 19 added judgement days each (round 22's count on the fixed schedule; the dry runs give the exact count) and\n   153\tthe dependency block's prompt growth (about 10%): about $1.05. Three linked and three controls: about $5.8, plus retries and refusals\n   154\t(about 5%) and the live reservations (one judge call per sequence at once). Priced exactly from the dry runs before the ceiling\n   155\tquestion.\n   156\t\n   157\t## 6. Time\n   158\t\n   159\tOne paid call at a time per sequence: about 270–300 calls; old-2's stored reader calls took a median 38.2 seconds (round 23), so about\n   160\t3 hours with replay and retries, the six sequences at once.\n   161\t\n   162\t## 7. Round 22 findings and where they are answered\n   163\t\n   164\t1. Fresh judgements by date, request families, latest available upstream conclusion, the stored refusals: §1 (fresh judgements), §2,\n   165\t   §3 items 2–3. 2. P on annual ranges, carry-forward, coverage, all three pairs, outcomes for every case, the parser validated on\n   166\t   flagged and unflagged stored answers: §4, `parser-validation.md`. 3. H4 on every post-link v14 answer; H3 traced outside the\n   167\t   dependency block; H5 with an effect on the verdict; per-link report; refusals with a denominator, stops apart: §4. 4. July's latch:\n   168\t   §1, §2, §6.\n   169\tRound 23: freshness on the archive side, admission bound to the judging day, the upstream snapshot at prompt construction with its three\n   170\tcases: §2, §3 item 2. Every distinct pair hand-classified, misparses recorded, carried-forward dates: §4. Paired rates on jointly\n   171\treadable dates of the fixed 73, unresolved dates unable to reverse, conversions to overlap reported apart: §4 P. H3 traced through the\n   172\tlink to the outside statement: §4 H3. H1 second refusal, H2 invalidates, H4 and H5 consequences in P, disagreement resolution,\n   173\tverdict precedence: §4. Time: §6.\n   174\tRound 24: three outcomes per pair from two-sided bounds over all 73 dates, the floor on known control dates, 60 stated as a policy: §4 P.\n   175\tProduct against infrastructure failure, matched reader-days, the H1 tolerance's rationale: §4 H1 and precedence. Credit only for gains\n   176\tthat pass rule B, the CPU increment shown removed or the pair undecided, H3 triggers on amount, scope or attribution against the\n   177\tcontrol's conclusion in force: §4. The worksheet, its worked example, adjudication for P, H3, H4 and H5, reading time: §4.\n   178\tRound 25: every gain of a provisionally improved pair reviewed, both supplying answers, deduplicated by judgement id; only gains\n   179\tadjusted, by one credit rule; H4 and H5 reach P only through it; H1's boundary stated (harm at 4); reading load counted per reader:\n   180\t§4. The harness review will run two $0 verdict checks: a pair whose control has 2 definite dates and the linked arm none (both gains\n   181\treviewed, withdrawing both gives a tie: not improved); a material error shared by both arms on a clear date (no change to P).\n     1\t**NOT READY.** Version 4 fixes the handling of unresolved dates and linked double refusals. The remaining blocker is how P gives or withdraws credit for a gain. Two readers can apply the current wording to the same forecasts and reach opposite verdicts. [Credit rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:79) · [H4 and H5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:114)\n     2\t\n     3\t### Blocking changes\n     4\t\n     5\t1. **Review every credited gain in a provisionally improved pair.** “A gain needed for improved” leaves the review set open. If the control has two definite dates and the linked arm has zero, withdrawing either gain alone still leaves an improvement. Withdrawing both produces a tie. A reader who tests whether each gain is individually necessary could skip both. Require Rule B review of both supplying forecasts for every credited gain, deduplicated by judgement ID, then recompute P. [Credit rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:88) · [July Rule B](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/reading-rule.md:8)\n     6\t2. **Use one rule for withdrawing credit.** H5 currently turns affected linked overlap or clear dates into definite dates, even where the control was also clear. A material error shared by both arms could therefore make linked P fail without any difference on those dates. Limit P adjustments to actual gains. For H4, say explicitly that removing a quantified unsupported CPU increment determines the **credit-adjusted** class. If the increment cannot be quantified and could decide the verdict, use the unresolved bounds. Keep the original forecast class, and keep confirmed double counting as harm. The fixed H5 sample still supplies its separate paired harm count. [Credit rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:88) · [H4 and H5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:114)\n     7\t\n     8\t### Round-24 findings that hold as fixed\n     9\t\n    10\t- **Bounds and precedence.** The two-sided bounds preserve known classes on all 73 dates. The round-24 example with 13 unresolved linked dates now returns undecided. The two-day floor uses known control dates, and **60 resolved dates per arm** is an explicit coverage policy. A linked double refusal now fails before the generic stop rule. H1 uses matched reader-days, so propagation-only days cannot dilute it. The written threshold tolerates **exactly three** excess refusals and fails at four. Align the rationale with that boundary. [Bounds and coverage](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:79) · [H1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:95) · [Verdict precedence](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:123)\n    11\t- **Sourcing and worksheet.** H3 now catches a changed scope or attribution at the same dollar amount and compares the specific error with the control conclusion in force that day. The worksheet specifies the judgement IDs, date spans, forecast excerpts, source traces, and adjudication. The $419B versus $414–421B example gives readers a concrete overlap case. In the dry review, count the second reader’s work separately from the number of distinct rows, and include H3 and the full gain review in the workload estimate. [H3](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:105) · [Worksheet](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:128)\n    12\t\n    13\tSettle the two credit rules before building. The harness review can then use the two examples above as $0 verdict checks.","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"**NOT READY.** Version 5 fixes both round-25 counterexamples. One ambiguity remains in how the credit rule handles multiple unquantifiable CPU gains. Two readers can still reach **pass** and **inconclusive** from the same answers. [Credit rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:89)\n\n### Round-25 findings\n\nBoth fixes hold. Every gain in a provisionally improved pair now gets Rule B review of both supplying judgements. If the control has two definite dates and both linked gains lose credit, the result is a tie. Only gain dates can be adjusted, so a material error shared by both arms on a clear date leaves P unchanged. H4 double counting and the H5 sample retain their separate harm checks. Both verdict checks are specified in §7. [Credit rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:89) · [H4 and H5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:119) · [Verdict checks](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:178)\n\nH1 now explicitly tolerates three excess refusals and fails at four. The worksheet counts the two reads separately and includes H3 and the full gain review. Those findings are closed. [H1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:100) · [Reading load](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:131)\n\n### Exact remaining change\n\nRemove the per-gain condition **“and could decide the pair.”** Suppose a control has two definite dates and the linked arm has none, with both gains resting on unquantifiable unsupported CPU increments. Marking either gain unresolved by itself still leaves an improvement, since **1 < 2**. Marking both unresolved makes the pair **undecided**, since **2 < 2** is false. Mark **every** such gain unresolved, then recompute the bounds once across the pair. [Bounds](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:80) · [CPU credit rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:93)\n\nState that the **60-date coverage check uses the original hand-read classes**, while the bounds use the credit-adjusted classes. A readable forecast with unsupported arithmetic still counts as readable, and its uncertainty still affects P. Add the two-unquantifiable-gains case to the $0 verdict checks. [Coverage rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:86) · [Verdict precedence](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:126)"}}
